{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall not eat with it leavened bread.</b> With it, as in 'upon its legs and upon its innards' — you shall not slaughter upon leavened bread the blood of my sacrifice." "<b>Seven days you shall eat with it</b> etc. On its account, as in 'For your sake we are killed all the day' (Psalms 44:23), 'for your sake we bear reproach' (ibid.) — meaning: you shall eat matzot on account of the sacrifice that comes as a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt." "<b>Bread of affliction.</b> The way of a poor person — when they give him a little flour, he has no vessel to heat the water in to knead it, and no leisure to let it leaven; rather, he mixes it with a little cold water and throws it into the oven or onto the hearth." "<b>For in haste you went out of the land of Egypt.</b> This tells you that the Holy One, blessed be He, could have left them in Egypt until their dough leavened, and they could prepare provisions for themselves. Rather, it was only so that it be a remembrance of the matter — therefore 'seven days you shall eat matzot' etc." "<b>So that you remember the day of your going out</b> etc. Therefore we recite the passage of tzitzit morning and evening, because we are obligated to mention the Exodus from Egypt by day and by night, as we learned at the end of chapter 1 of Berakhot: Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah said: I am as one seventy years old, and I did not merit that the Exodus from Egypt be mentioned at night, until Ben Zoma expounded it, etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:02.958947393Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}